<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Toul</h1><p class="st-meta">928 CE – 1648 CE (approx.) · capital Toul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The smallest of the Three Bishoprics of Lorraine, an ecclesiastical principality of the Empire that produced a reforming pope and was absorbed by France.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a> had been a bishopric since late Roman times. In the 10th century, as the German kings sought loyal counterweights to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Lorraine</a> nobility, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> received comital rights over the city and its surroundings, traditionally dated to 928 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>, making them princes of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> ruled a small territory on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>, often clashing with the dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Lorraine</a> and with the citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a>, who won considerable self-government. Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-ix">Bruno of Egisheim</a> became Pope Leo IX in 1049 and drove the Gregorian reform. The diocese itself was far larger than the temporal principality.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1552 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> as 'protector' during his war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. French control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-bishoprics">Three Bishoprics</a> was confirmed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648, and Toul became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-toul.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Toul" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: OwenBlacker · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trois-%C3%89v%C3%AAch%C3%A9s.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>928 CE – 1648 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Toul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gauzelin of Toul, Bruno of Egisheim (Pope Leo IX)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bishopric of Toul, Hochstift Toul</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1288&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-toul" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Toul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4816 477 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Toul lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5136 733L5229 694M5136 733L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5136 733h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5136 733h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1288&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-toul">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

